# MC-E1-F2, the other places a period word stands. L §2.2: period words are "an index's period kind (day, month,
# year, §4.1), and the period argument of periodic and factor"; L §4.1 durations are "an integer and a period word";
# L §6.1 a member's items mean what they mean alone. The member declares indexes named month, day and year; joined, its
# periodic(r, month, …), factor(r, n, year, …), `d0 - 1 day` and its index `day` read as alone: holes of the inputs
# they read, and g at the key 2026-01-05 of its own `day`.
=== j/joint.tally
model j
  tally 2
  on 2026-01-01
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2026-12-31
  use m = "../m" sha256 "83ae75170e505b3a947608cbe4853dd993061c5afb42dbc2f409be5154bbbdbe"

ask jq: m.p, m.f, m.e, m.g[2026-01-05] law 2026-06-30
=== m/model.tally
model m
  tally 2
  on 2026-01-01
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2026-12-31

index month = month 2026-01..2026-12
index day = day 2026-01-01..2026-01-31
index year = year 2026..2027

input r: rate question "r?"

def p: num = periodic(r, month, nominal 12)
input n: int question "n?"
def f: num = factor(r, n, year, effective)
input d0: date question "d?"
def e: date = d0 - 1 day
def g[k in day]: date = k

ask q: p, e law 2026-06-30
=== expect jq
jq: (?"r?" (m.r), ?"n?" (m.n) ?"r?" (m.r), ?"d?" (m.d0), 2026-01-05)
  law date 2026-06-30, cut 2026-01-01
